(7597703, 1226401, 63218, '<td valign="top">Great idea, (great gardening skills too). Fearless execution. The result -- a great shot &amp; a new PB! Congratulations!</td>', 'Great idea, (great gardening skills too). Fearless execution. The result -- a great shot & a new PB! Congratulations!', '2018-09-26 13:36:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(7597704, 1226281, 2333, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by scalvert:</b></div><hr/><i> Wow, 13 years, and your last ribbon was a photo of stars, too. Obviously you should get outside more often. I''m impressed that you dared to venture into Pixinsight''s deep, muddy waters.<br/> <br/>Here''s a little trick you might find useful... in Photoshop, select Color Range &gt; Highlights with a pretty big fuzziness value to catch most of the stars. Select &gt; Modify &gt; Expand by 4 or 5 pixels, then hide the selection. Choose Filter &gt; Other &gt; Minimum with a radius of about 0.4 pixels (preserve roundness), and see if that helps. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Ha, yes I do need to get out more, LOL. I finally have a UWA lens that I''m really satisfied with for astro work. Pixinsight has been "fun" to learn, the user interface can be quite daunting to the uninitiated. Thank goodness for lightvortexastronomy.com!</td>', 'Originally posted by scalvert: Wow, 13 years, and your last ribbon was a photo of stars, too. Obviously you should get outside more often. I''m impressed that you dared to venture into Pixinsight''s deep, muddy waters. Here''s a little trick you might find useful... in Photoshop, select Color Range > Highlights with a pretty big fuzziness value to catch most of the stars. Select > Modify > Expand by 4 or 5 pixels, then hide the selection. Choose Filter > Other > Minimum with a radius of about 0.4 pixels (preserve roundness), and see if that helps.   Ha, yes I do need to get out more, LOL. I finally have a UWA lens that I''m really satisfied with for astro work. Pixinsight has been "fun" to learn, the user interface can be quite daunting to the uninitiated. Thank goodness for lightvortexastronomy.com!', '2018-09-26 14:25:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(7597708, 1226401, 19825, '<td valign="top">Stunning capture and unique point of view. This is one of the reasons I love DPC. It gives us an excuse to go out to our gardens, lie on our backs, gaze up at the puffy clouds, and, occasionally, capture magic. Congrats on the new PB, Nikki.</td>', 'Stunning capture and unique point of view. This is one of the reasons I love DPC. It gives us an excuse to go out to our gardens, lie on our backs, gaze up at the puffy clouds, and, occasionally, capture magic. Congrats on the new PB, Nikki.', '2018-09-26 16:10:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(7597716, 1226281, 87808, '<td valign="top">Absolutely gorgeous!  People find it hard to believe that there are really that many stars when you look up in a an AZ sky! You have a fabulous shot!</td>', 'Absolutely gorgeous!  People find it hard to believe that there are really that many stars when you look up in a an AZ sky! You have a fabulous shot!', '2018-09-26 18:48:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(7597721, 1226401, 43463, '<td valign="top">Way to go Nikki! Congrats on a new personal best, winning the blue, and capturing an absolutely magical photo! And here I was guessing it was accomplished with the flowers on a patio glass table top, but not so. Wow, nearly 7 feet tall zinnas; now that is a garden to get pleasantly lost in!!! Congrats again!</td>', 'Way to go Nikki! Congrats on a new personal best, winning the blue, and capturing an absolutely magical photo! And here I was guessing it was accomplished with the flowers on a patio glass table top, but not so. Wow, nearly 7 feet tall zinnas; now that is a garden to get pleasantly lost in!!! Congrats again!', '2018-09-26 21:18:06.000000', NULL, 0),
